{:edb/db-before-t 1710 :edb/db-after-t 1711 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535023 50 #inst "2026-10-08T14:57:21.948Z" 13194139535023 true] [17592186179462 1100 "The growth rate of the hare population $P(t)$ in a particular forest is jointly proportional to the hare population at time $t$, measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the hare population. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,01$ and the carrying capacity is $10\\,000$. How many hares will there be in the forest after $7$ years given that there were $2\\,500$ hares initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139535023 false] [17592186179462 1100 "The growth rate of the hare population $P(t)$ in a particular forest is jointly proportional to the hare population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the hare population. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,01$ and the carrying capacity is $10\\,000.$ How many hares will there be in the forest after $7$ years given that there were $2\\,500$ hares initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139535023 true] [17592186179462 1145 "Since the hare population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hare population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hare population, the hare population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality\n\n$$\nk=0.000\\,01\n$$\n\nand the carrying capacity is $a=10\\,000$. Therefore, the hare population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.000\\,01 P(10\\,000-P)\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{10\\,000}{1+Ce^{-(10\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,01)t}}= \\dfrac{10\\,000}{1+Ce^{-0.1t}}\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition\n\n$$\nP(0)=2\\,500\n$$\n\nand solve for $C$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0) &= 2500 \\\\\n(10000)/(1 + Ce^{-0.1(0)}) &= 2500 \\\\\n\\frac{10000}{1 + C} &= 2500 \\\\\n\\frac{1 + C}{10000} &= \\frac{1}{2500} \\\\\n1 + C &= \\frac{10000}{2500} \\\\\n1 + C &= 4 \\\\\nC &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\frac{10\\,000}{1+3 e^{-0.1 t}}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of hares in the forest after $7$ years, we substitute $t=7$ into the expression for $P(t)$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(7) &= \\frac{10000}{1 + 3e^{-0.1 \\cdot 7}} \\\\\n&\\approx 4016\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535023 false] [17592186179462 1145 "Since the hare population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hare population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hare population, the hare population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality $k=0.000\\,01,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=10\\,000.$ Therefore, the hare population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.000\\,01 P(10\\,000-P) .\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) \n= \\dfrac{10\\,000}{1+Ce^{-(10\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,01)t}}= \\dfrac{10\\,000}{1+Ce^{-0.1t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=2\\,500$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =2\\,500 \\\\ \\frac{10\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.1(0)}} & =2\\,500 \\\\ \\frac{10\\,000}{1+C} & =2\\,500 \\\\ \\frac{1+C}{10\\,000} & =\\frac{1}{2\\,500} \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{10\\,000}{2\\,500} \\\\ 1+C & =4 \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\frac{10\\,000}{1+3 e^{-0.1 t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of hares in the forest after $7$ years, we substitute $t=7$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(7) & =\\frac{10\\,000}{1+3{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 7}} \\\\  & ≈4\\,016.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535023 true] [17592186179463 1100 "The rate of growth of the panda population $P(t)$ in a particular forest follows the logistic growth model is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.001\\,25 P(800-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the beginning of the observations. Find an expression for the panda population after $t$ years, given that there were $400$ pandas initially." 13194139535023 true] [17592186179463 1100 "The rate of growth of the panda population $P(t)$ in a particular forest follows the logistic growth model is\n\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.001\\,25 P(800-P)$,\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the beginning of the observations. Find an expression for the panda population after $t$ years, given that there were $400$ pandas initially." 13194139535023 false] [17592186179463 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is\n\n$$\nk=0.001\\,25\n$$\n\nand the carrying capacity is $a=800$.\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{800}{1+Ce^{-(800\\cdot 0.001\\,25)t}}= \\dfrac{800}{1+Ce^{-t}}\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition\n\n$$\nP(0)=400\n$$\n\nand solve for $C$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{800}{1 + Ce^{-0}} &= 400 \\\\\n\\frac{800}{1 + C} &= 400 \\\\\n\\frac{1 + C}{800} &= \\frac{1}{400} \\\\\n1 + C &= \\frac{800}{400} \\\\\nC &= 2 - 1 \\\\\nC &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the panda population is given by\n\n$$\nP(t)= \\dfrac{800}{1+e^{-t}}\n$$" 13194139535023 false] [17592186179463 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.001\\,25,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=800.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) \n= \\dfrac{800}{1+Ce^{-(800\\cdot 0.001\\,25)t}}= \\dfrac{800}{1+Ce^{-t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=400$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-0}} & =400 \\\\ \\frac{800}{1+C} & =400 \\\\ \\frac{1+C}{800} & =\\frac{1}{400} \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{800}{400} \\\\ C & =2-1 \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the panda population is given by\n\n$$\nP(t)= \\dfrac{800}{1+e^{-t}} .\n$$" 13194139535023 true] [17592186179606 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327352 13194139535023 true] [17592186179606 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327359 13194139535023 true] [17592186179606 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327380 13194139535023 true] [17592186179606 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327394 13194139535023 true] [17592186179607 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327345 13194139535023 true] [17592186179607 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327362 13194139535023 true] [17592186179607 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327366 13194139535023 true] [17592186179607 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327373 13194139535023 true] [17592186179607 1020 #edb/ref 17592186327387 13194139535023 true] [17592186180224 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535023 true] [17592186180224 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe growth rate of the squirrel population $P(t)$ in a certain forest is jointly proportional to the squirrel population at time $t$, measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the squirrel population. The constant of proportionality is $0.00001$ and the carrying capacity is $5000$. How many squirrels will there be in the forest after $10$ years given that there were $4000$ squirrels initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139535023 false] [17592186180224 1100 "The growth rate of the squirrel population $P(t)$ in a certain forest is jointly proportional to the squirrel population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the squirrel population. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,01$ and the carrying capacity is $5000.$ How many squirrels will there be in the forest after $10$ years given that there were $4000$ squirrels initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139535023 true] [17592186180224 1145 "Since the squirrel population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the squirrel population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the squirrel population, the squirrel population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,01,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=5000.$ Therefore, the squirrel population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,01P(5000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5000}{1+C{e}^{-(5000\\cdot 0.000\\,01)t}}=\\frac{5000}{1+C{e}^{-0.05t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=4000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =4000 \\\\ \\frac{5000}{1+C{e}^{-0.05(0)}} & =4000 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ C & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5000}{1+0.25{e}^{-0.05\\cdot t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=10$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =\\frac{5000}{1+0.25{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 10}} \\\\  & ≈4342.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327345 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327346 13194139535023 true] [17592186327345 1089 "q-119861" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327345 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535023 true] [17592186327345 1100 "The rate of growth in the number of fishes $P(t)$ with a fungal infection in a certain pond is jointly proportional to the number of fishes that have been infected at time $t$ and the number that have not been infected. If the constant of proportionality is $0.009$ and the carrying capacity is $130,$ then find an expression for the number of fishes with fungal infection, given that there were $5$ fishes infected initially." 13194139535023 true] [17592186327345 1113 #uuid "2ca1449f-8949-5519-8f98-ed37dccf1a4d" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327345 1145 "Since the growth rate of infected fishes is *jointly* proportional to the number of fishes that have been infected at time $t,$ and the number that have not, the number of infected fishes follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.009,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=130.$ Therefore, the growth of the number of fishes with fungal infection is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.009P(130-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{130}{1+C{e}^{-(130\\cdot 0.009)t}}=\\frac{130}{1+C{e}^{-1.17t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=5$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{130}{1+C{e}^{-1.17\\cdot 0}} & =5 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{130}{5} \\\\ C & =26-1 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t)=\\frac{130}{1+25{e}^{-1.17t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327346 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327347 13194139535023 true] [17592186327346 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327348 13194139535023 true] [17592186327346 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327349 13194139535023 true] [17592186327346 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327350 13194139535023 true] [17592186327346 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327351 13194139535023 true] [17592186327346 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535023 true] [17592186327346 1041 "selection" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327346 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327351 13194139535023 true] [17592186327346 1110 #uuid "de450828-f588-5f1a-88f1-da16f9577e9d" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327347 1034 "P(t)=\\frac{25}{1+{e}^{-0.009t}}" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327347 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535023 true] [17592186327347 1111 #uuid "8912babc-6e4b-5f3e-912d-6a0065743b20" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327348 1034 "P(t)=\\frac{5}{1+25{e}^{-1.17t}}" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327348 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535023 true] [17592186327348 1111 #uuid "1e992a9d-587d-5289-ae53-0e7d820fc4e3" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327349 1034 "P(t)=\\frac{25}{1+{e}^{-t}}" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327349 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535023 true] [17592186327349 1111 #uuid "391985b4-fcd1-50ad-890f-1b7e26744141" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327350 1034 "P(t)=\\frac{130}{1+25{e}^{-0.009t}}" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327350 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535023 true] [17592186327350 1111 #uuid "e453d900-273f-5a1e-851e-0d6d5a484e2d" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327351 1034 "P(t)=\\frac{130}{1+25{e}^{-1.17t}}" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327351 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535023 true] [17592186327351 1111 #uuid "797ca0e7-6bec-5dab-9b05-d0a0c20f2ad7" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327352 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327353 13194139535023 true] [17592186327352 1089 "q-120253" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327352 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535023 true] [17592186327352 1100 "The growth rate in the number $P(t)$ of files infected with a virus in a computer is jointly proportional to the number of infected files at time $t,$ measured in hours, and the difference between the computer's carrying capacity and the number of infected files. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,005$ and the carrying capacity is $100\\,000.$ How many files will have the virus after $12$ hours given that there were $1000$ files with the virus initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139535023 true] [17592186327352 1113 #uuid "01e090ea-18cf-5d07-9563-7039d4d1d497" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327352 1145 "Since the number of files infected with the virus' growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the number of infected files at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the number of infected files, the number of files infected with the virus follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=100\\,000.$ Therefore, the growth of the number of files infected with the virus is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,005P(100\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(100\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,005)t}}=\\frac{100\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =1000 \\\\ \\frac{100\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5(0)}} & =1000 \\\\ 1+C & =100 \\\\ C & =99.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100\\,000}{1+99{e}^{-0.5\\cdot t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=12$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =\\frac{100\\,000}{1+99{e}^{-0.5\\cdot 12}} \\\\  & ≈80\\,296.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327353 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327354 13194139535023 true] [17592186327353 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327355 13194139535023 true] [17592186327353 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327356 13194139535023 true] [17592186327353 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327357 13194139535023 true] [17592186327353 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327358 13194139535023 true] [17592186327353 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535023 true] [17592186327353 1041 "selection" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327353 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327355 13194139535023 true] [17592186327353 1110 #uuid "49671687-4a8b-5d56-906f-715ca3c0205c" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327354 1034 "100\\,000" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327354 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535023 true] [17592186327354 1111 #uuid "bc5a3bd2-32fd-50bc-b18d-9843dbda43a1" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327355 1034 "80\\,296" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327355 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535023 true] [17592186327355 1111 #uuid "8f6b953d-3ac6-54e5-a1c4-5579ce19b0ff" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327356 1034 "1\\,638" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327356 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535023 true] [17592186327356 1111 #uuid "54a2609e-7724-5d06-8c34-e5b59cc61ae6" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327357 1034 "5\\,000" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327357 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535023 true] [17592186327357 1111 #uuid "6ecd2d6f-e151-5d0b-ba28-6a81c1980514" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327358 1034 "99" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327358 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535023 true] [17592186327358 1111 #uuid "18236403-e1d5-5d60-adba-83c15e215827" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327359 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327360 13194139535023 true] [17592186327359 1089 "q-347875" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327359 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535023 true] [17592186327359 1100 "The population $P(t)$ of rabbits on a particular island follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P-0.0002{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. If there were $400$ rabbits on the island initially, how many rabbits inhabited the island after $3$ years? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAfter $3$ years, approximately\n{{field-1}} rabbits inhabited the island." 13194139535023 true] [17592186327359 1113 #uuid "ebc94644-35a7-5ccd-b4dd-ea6583f0712d" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327359 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.2P-0.0002{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0002P(1000-P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.0002,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1000.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1000}{1+C{e}^{-(1000\\cdot 0.0002)t}}=\\frac{1000}{1+C{e}^{-0.2t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=400$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1000}{1+C{e}^{-0.2(0)}} & =400 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{1000}{400} \\\\ C & =2.5-1 \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1000}{1+1.5{e}^{-0.2t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=3$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =\\frac{1000}{1+1.5{e}^{-0.2(3)}} \\\\  & ≈548.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $3$ years, approximately $548$ rabbits inhabited the island." 13194139535023 true] [17592186327360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327361 13194139535023 true] [17592186327360 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535023 true] [17592186327360 1041 "field-1" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327360 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327361 13194139535023 true] [17592186327360 1110 #uuid "746cee96-5e9f-5474-9c16-10527f2d168e" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327361 1034 "548" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327361 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535023 true] [17592186327361 1111 #uuid "0022dcba-6cad-574b-b71e-4fa8ab87afaa" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327362 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327363 13194139535023 true] [17592186327362 1089 "q-353253" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327362 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535023 true] [17592186327362 1100 "The growth rate of the number of residents using a new fitness app, $P(t),$ in a small town is jointly proportional to the number of residents using the app at time $t,$ measured in months, and the difference between the town’s maximum potential users and the number of residents currently using the app.\n\nGiven that the constant of proportionality is $0.005$ and the maximum potential number of users is $800,$ find an expression for the number of residents using the app after $t$ months, given that there were $100$ users initially.\n\n| $P(t)\\,=\\,\\,$ | $800$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1\\,+\\,$ {{field-1}} |  |" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327362 1113 #uuid "d3bdf0d0-1ee1-5303-bb41-ac3247396350" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327362 1145 "Since the number of residents using the app grows at a rate that is *jointly* proportional to the number of residents using the app at time $t$ and the difference between the maximum potential number of users and the number currently using the app, the number of residents using the app follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=800.$ Therefore, the growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.005\\,P(800-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-(800\\cdot 0.005)t}}=\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-4t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=100$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-4\\cdot 0}} & =100 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{800}{100} \\\\ C & =8-1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{800}{1+7{e}^{-4t}}.\n$$" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327363 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327364 13194139535023 true] [17592186327363 1000 #edb/ref 17592186327365 13194139535023 true] [17592186327363 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535023 true] [17592186327363 1041 "field-1" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327363 1104 #edb/ref 17592186327364 13194139535023 true] [17592186327363 1110 #uuid "10162ae4-12f0-5d53-a516-997edbc6bdb9" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327364 1034 "7e^{-4t}" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327364 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535023 true] [17592186327364 1111 #uuid "9d807166-1d92-5d35-8b97-350a0171ca76" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327365 1034 "7e^{-0.005t}" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327365 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535023 true] [17592186327365 1111 #uuid "b5b391eb-74f1-59d0-9560-7d57c357dc98" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327366 1017 #edb/ref 17592186327367 13194139535023 true] [17592186327366 1089 "q-83868" 13194139535023 true] [17592186327366 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535023 true] [17592186327366 1100 "The growth rate of the crab population $P(t)$ in a certain river is jointly proportional to the crab population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the river's carrying capacity and the crab population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,02$ and the carrying capacity is $10\\,000,$ then fi